    "description": "Research on women’s exercise in Iran identifies access to suitable spaces, usable opening hours, equipment, and trained staff as connected infrastructure issues. Local qualitative studies document barriers experienced by participants, but do not establish a national inventory of facilities available to women.",
    "tags": ["Women’s sport", "Sports facilities", "Public space", "Physical activity", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:43:29Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:43:29Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Women’s Sports Infrastructure in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "How facilities, opening hours, location, equipment, and staffing shape women’s access to sport in Iran, with clear limits on available evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "access-beyond-buildings",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access involves more than a building",
        "content": "For women seeking regular exercise, a sports facility’s existence does not establish whether it is practically accessible. Iranian qualitative research identifies scheduling, distance, cost, and the environment inside a venue as relevant barriers. These findings make access a question of how facilities operate as well as where they are built. [s1]\n\nA study published in 2018 described unsuitable venue schedules, ancillary facilities, gym space, and shortages of sports specialists among the obstacles reported by participants. One account contrasted women’s early-morning access with men’s evening use of a gym during hot weather. This illustrates how allocation of time can affect the usefulness of shared infrastructure; it does not establish a nationwide scheduling rule. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "local-experience",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What women in Kermanshah reported",
        "content": "A separate study interviewed 25 purposively selected women in Kermanshah, western Iran, in 2025. Participants described restricted access to well-equipped venues, inconvenient swimming-pool hours, and discomfort using public spaces dominated by men. Some also criticized enclosed women’s spaces for their condition or limited availability. These are accounts of lived experience, rather than an engineering inspection or representative survey of Iranian facilities. [s2]\n\nThe distinction matters for regional interpretation. Evidence from one city can identify problems worth investigating elsewhere, but cannot establish that every municipality provides the same facilities or follows the same allocation practices. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-infrastructure",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the evidence can establish",
        "content": "Together, these studies support examining usable access alongside construction: opening hours, affordability, location, equipment, and staffing all deserve attention. They do not quantify how many women can obtain a suitable session near home, or how that access has changed annually. A national assessment would therefore need operating information alongside a facility count. This is an editorial implication of the evidence, not a finding that a comprehensive monitoring system already exists. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
