    "description": "Research on women in Iranian sports management examines promotion, organizational culture, and access to leadership. Available studies distinguish women’s qualifications and experience from the institutional conditions affecting their authority and advancement.",
    "tags": ["Women’s sport", "Sports management", "Leadership", "Gender equality", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:43:29Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:43:29Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Women’s Sports Management in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "What research reveals about women’s promotion and leadership in Iranian sports organizations, and what remains unverified."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "promotion-and-organization",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Qualifications and opportunities",
        "content": "A study published in 2016 examined women’s position in Iranian sports organizations using questionnaire data collected in 2009. Its abstract reported relatively stronger assessments of several personal and professional factors, including education, organizational commitment, information-technology skills, and management skills, alongside a weaker assessment of the organizational environment. The authors argued for changes supporting women managers. [s1]\n\nThis distinction is useful: developing an individual’s skills and changing the conditions under which organizations promote staff are separate tasks. However, the study’s older evidence cannot establish the composition of leadership in 2026. The inspected abstract also does not provide a current national denominator against which to calculate women’s representation. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "recent-leadership-research",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Challenges at several levels",
        "content": "A 2025 study approached leadership through interviews with 10 women who had at least five years of managerial experience in Iranian sports organizations. Interviews took place between November 2022 and June 2023. The accessible abstract grouped challenges into societal, organizational, and individual levels. [s2]\n\nReported organizational challenges included discrimination in selection, weaknesses in educational strategies, and appointments described as symbolic. The authors also identified cultural conditions and personal-life pressures. These are themes derived from experienced managers’ interviews, not measured rates of discrimination or findings about every sports organization. Only the abstract was inspected, so detailed case histories and the full analysis cannot be reconstructed here. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessing-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to assess management change",
        "content": "Read together, the studies suggest that leadership should be examined through both career development and organizational opportunity. An appointment alone cannot reveal the scope of a manager’s decision-making power. For this guide, a stronger assessment would require current appointment rules, defined responsibilities, staffing information, and evidence of implementation. That is an editorial research requirement arising from the studies, rather than a verified description of a particular federation’s governance. [s1][s2]\n\nThe available evidence supports a focused account of barriers to advancement. It does not yet support a national organizational chart, a current leadership percentage, or a claim that recent appointments have resolved the reported problems.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
